# class Tree { k = 0; again(): Tree { return new (this.constructor as any)(); } }
# class Ash extends Tree { k = 1; }
# class Birch extends Tree { k = 20; }
# function count(turns: number, one: Tree, other: Tree) { let sum = 0; for (let i = 0; i < turns; i++) { const from = i % 3 ? one : other; sum += from.again().k; } return sum; }
# print(count(6, new Ash(), new Birch()), count(3, new Birch(), new Tree()));
# -- end of source
# f1 = count
# Every prototype object is made with `constructor`, which holds the class object. Off an object of a class it is read like a method declared above: behind the looks at the cell and the guard of the name. Off the few classes of a row a test of the shape tells them apart, and each arm constructs its own class in place.
